# mvnw and dev-server.sh are POSIX shell scripts. CRLF breaks their shebang.
mvnw        text eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf
mvnw.cmd    text eol=crlf

# *** SOURCE AND CONTENT ARE TEXT BY DECLARATION, NOT BY AUTO-DETECTION. ***
#
# Without these lines, "every committed blob is LF" rests on core.autocrlf -- a
# PER-CLONE setting nobody can see in review. Measured 2026-09-20: it is `true`
# in this clone's LOCAL config, global unset, so for a .java file the protection
# was one uncommitted setting on one machine.
#
# `text`, NOT `text eol=lf`, and the difference is the point: `text` pins the
# INDEX to LF and leaves the working tree to the platform, which is what keeps
# CRLF checkouts on Windows and LF on Linux. The three rules above use `eol=lf`
# because their WORKING TREE shape is what matters -- a CRLF shebang does not run.
#
# MEASURED BEFORE PINNING, so none of these carries a renormalisation commit.
# `git ls-files --eol` over the whole repo at `6c4a563`:
#
#       698  i/lf
#         1  i/-text        <- the fixture below, excluded on the next line
#         0  i/crlf         <- nothing to renormalise
#
# Reported as the WHOLE distribution rather than as an lf-vs-crlf pair: the
# instrument is THREE-valued, and reading it as two is exactly what would have
# renormalised the fixture without anyone noticing.
#
# `*.yml` and `*.md` are in scope because they were measured, not because they
# seemed safe. A type nobody has read does not get a line here.
#
# EIGHT TRACKED FILES ARE LEFT UNPINNED, AND THAT IS UNRULED RATHER THAN
# EXCLUDED: the four `pom.xml`, `.gitignore`, this file, `maven-wrapper.properties`
# and `golden-lore.txt`. All eight measure `i/lf` today, so pinning them would be
# safe -- nobody has decided whether to, and the question is open rather than
# closed. `git ls-files --eol | awk '{print $3}' | sort | uniq -c` lists them as
# `attr/`.
*.java      text
*.yml       text
*.md        text

# *** AND THE ONE FILE WHOSE BYTE SHAPE IS LOAD-BEARING. THIS LINE IS THE SAME
# FIX, NOT AN EXCEPTION CARVED OUT OF IT. ***
#
# EnchantCodecTest carries a deliberate NUL byte inside a string literal, so git
# auto-detects it as binary (`i/-text`). That is CORRECT, and it is the fixture
# CLAUDE.md's `grep -rho` entry rests on. Measured on this file, 2026-09-20:
#
#     git grep -c '@Test'   ->  15      (git reads the index, sees the text)
#     grep -rho  '@Test'    ->   1      (GNU grep 3.0's "Binary file ... matches"
#                                        notice, written to stdout and counted)
#     grep -rhoa '@Test'    ->  15      (-a forces text; the 15 reappear)
#
# `*.java text` above would force text handling onto it and destroy that.
#
# *** THE LINE CHANGES NOTHING TODAY, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS WORTH HAVING. *** It
# writes down a property currently held by a HEURISTIC: auto-detection scans only
# about the first 8000 bytes, so an edit that pushes the NUL past that window
# would silently reclassify the file as text -- no error, nothing in a diff, and
# the `grep -rho` entry quietly stops being reproducible.
#
# Last match wins in this file, so this MUST stay below `*.java text`.
core/src/test/java/io/github/butterflysmp/rpg/core/enchant/EnchantCodecTest.java -text
